{#
  The Race Overview, LaTeX family. One Race's whole catalogue as four flat,
  cross-linked sections — Units, Models, Equipment, Spawns — followed by the
  Rules Reference (ADR 0031). A fielded Army has one chosen path through Unit,
  Model and Equipment, which is why the Army Reference nests them; a Race is an
  N:M web, so every record prints exactly once here and the links carry the
  structure instead.

  The Markdown twin at `markdown/race-overview/main.md.jinja` renders the same
  document: the same sections in the same order, the same records, and the same
  cross-links. Only the link and table syntax is this family's own (ADR 0005).

  Each section opens with a summary table whose first cell links into the
  record's own entry below it. A catalogue's table runs past one page, so those
  are `longtable`s with a repeating header rather than `tabular`s.

  A record's entry is a sectioning command, so a plain `\label` after it
  records that very counter. `\phantomsection` is needed only where there is no
  sectioning command for the label to sit on — a Spawn's table row — and inside
  the Rules Reference partial, whose entries are bold text.

  Designer-authored Markdown prose (`lore`, `tip`, the notes) goes through
  `md_to_latex`; short own-strings — a size label, a holder, a Type — take
  `latex_escape` (ADR 0005's addendum).
-#}
\BLOCK{macro links(items)}\BLOCK{for link in items}\BLOCK{if not loop.first}, \BLOCK{endif}\hyperref[\VAR{link.anchor}]{\VAR{link.name | latex_escape}}\BLOCK{endfor}\BLOCK{endmacro}
{#- A rule's name, linked when the Rules Reference holds its entry. -#}
\BLOCK{macro rule(line)}\BLOCK{if line.anchor}\hyperref[\VAR{line.anchor}]{\VAR{line.name | latex_escape}}\BLOCK{else}\VAR{line.name | latex_escape}\BLOCK{endif}\BLOCK{endmacro}
{#- One Special per `\item`, for a list the caller has already opened. -#}
\BLOCK{macro special_items(lines)}
\BLOCK{for line in lines}
\item \textbf{\VAR{rule(line)}}\VAR{': ' ~ (line.text | latex_escape) if line.text else ''}
\BLOCK{endfor}
\BLOCK{endmacro}
{#- One Slot's Specials, under the label naming what the Slot grants. Slots
    stay apart: what a record grants its Unit and what it is itself are
    different claims. -#}
\BLOCK{macro slot_specials(label, lines)}
\BLOCK{if lines}
\item \textit{\VAR{label | latex_escape}:}
\begin{itemize}
\VAR{special_items(lines)}
\end{itemize}
\BLOCK{endif}
\BLOCK{endmacro}
{#- Declared deltas: a Stacker is printed as the change it declares, never
    resolved, because it has no value until a Unit is fielded under it. -#}
\BLOCK{macro deltas(label, modifiers)}
\BLOCK{if modifiers}
\item \textit{\VAR{label | latex_escape}:}
\begin{itemize}
\BLOCK{for modifier in modifiers}
\item \VAR{modifier | latex_escape}
\BLOCK{endfor}
\end{itemize}
\BLOCK{endif}
\BLOCK{endmacro}
{#- The cost cells of a summary row, one column per currency. -#}
\BLOCK{macro cost_cells(columns)}\BLOCK{for cell in columns} & \VAR{cell}\BLOCK{endfor}\BLOCK{endmacro}
{#- One order-type as a flat table, a row per option. The widest row fixes the
    column count, so a shorter one is padded rather than shifting the table. -#}
\BLOCK{macro order_table(title, rows)}
\BLOCK{if rows}
\BLOCK{set ncols = (rows | map('last') | map('length') | list | max)}

\noindent\textbf{\VAR{title | latex_escape}}

\begin{tabular}{l\VAR{'l' * ncols}}
\BLOCK{for speed, cells in rows}
\VAR{speed | latex_escape}\BLOCK{for cell in cells} & \VAR{cell | latex_escape}\BLOCK{endfor}\BLOCK{for _ in range(ncols - cells | length)} & \BLOCK{endfor} \\
\BLOCK{endfor}
\end{tabular}
\vspace{1ex}
\BLOCK{endif}
\BLOCK{endmacro}
